{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>There is none like El, O Yeshurun.</b> After he finished blessing the tribes one by one, he now began to speak to all of them collectively, and to inform them of the goodness of their portion — for He is the Former of all. And he gives them glad tidings of the inheritance of the land and that they will merit to enter into it, which he himself did not merit, and he further explains to them the matter of the World to Come, as I shall explain, and he seals his words with matters such as these. Therefore he says, אין כאל ישרון — 'there is none like El, O Yeshurun' — that you are not like the Canaanites, for they are under the governance of the angels, the princes on high; but not so Israel, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 33:16): ״ונפלינו אני ועמך״ — 'and I and Your people shall be distinguished.' And therefore he called them Yeshurun, for it is a name of uprightness (ישר), to indicate that among all the princes on high there is none like the Elohim of Israel. And so he said of Israel (Deuteronomy 33:29): אשריך ישראל מי כמוך — 'happy are you, O Israel, who is like you' — for they are not like the seventy nations. Therefore he informs them that our portion is not assigned like theirs, nor our lot like all their multitudes. This is אין כאל ישרון — 'there is none like El, O Yeshurun' — who is רוכב שמים, 'who rides the heavens,' and He shall be found in your help, ובגאותו שחקים — 'and in His majesty, the skies' — in His majesty He rides the skies." "<b>And the sense of 'who rides the heavens.'</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): He calls governance by the term 'riding,' in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 32:13): ״ירכיבהו על במתי ארץ״ — 'He made him ride on the high places of the earth' — which is an expression of governance. Furthermore, to explain: just as a rider prevails over the horse, because the horse's running is not from its own power but from the power of the rider, in the manner of what is written (Job 39:18): ״תשחק לסוס ולרכבו״ — 'she laughs at the horse and his rider' — so too the running of the spheres and their motion is not from their own power but from the power of the Mover who moves them, may He be exalted. And the explanation of the word השמים — 'the heavens' — the intent is upon ערבות (the plains/firmament), and so our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed: ערבות — from where do we know? For it is called שמים, as it is written here: ״רוכב שמים״, and it is written there (Psalms 68:5): ״סולו לרוכב בערבות״ — 'sing to Him who rides upon the ערבות.'" "<b>And the Master,</b> Rabbi Moses of blessed memory, wrote in the Book of the Guide [of the Perplexed]: reflect upon what our Rabbis of blessed memory said — ערבות רם ונשא שוכן עליו — 'the ערבות are high and exalted; He dwells upon them' — as it is said: ״סולו לרוכב בערבות״. And they did not say שוכן בו — 'dwells in it' — for had they said so, it would have entailed for the Holy One, blessed be He, a place, or that the Name, may He be blessed, be a force among the forces, as the camps of the { philosophers } imagined. But in their saying שוכן עליו — 'dwells upon it' — they showed what is fitting: that He, blessed be He, is separate from the sphere and is not a force within it. For רוכב שמים — 'rider of the heavens' — its meaning is: He who causes the encompassing sphere to revolve and moves it according to His will, along with the rest of the spheres that are moved by its motion — and they move with the motion of a part within the whole. And this is the mighty power that moves all — end of quote." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: רוכב שמים — He called the attributes שמים. And the measure of the verse is: when Israel do the will of the Place (HaMakom), behold He is רוכב שמים — riding the heavens — and does not withdraw from His attributes. And when they do not do the will of the Place — ובגאותו שחקים — 'and in His majesty, the skies' — behold, He grows exalted and withdraws to the utmost heights, and He withdraws from His attributes. And similar to this our Rabbis of blessed memory drashed: when Israel do the will of the Place, they add strength to the supernal Gevurah, as it is said (Psalms 60:14): ״באלהים נעשה חיל״ — 'through Elohim we do valiantly' — and at a time when they do not do His will (Deuteronomy 32:18): ״צור ילדך תשי״ — 'the Rock that begot you, you disregard.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {2 "Reading כתות הצבא\"ה as a reference to philosophical schools, likely צבא השמים camps or simply philosophical sects; rendered literally as the text stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:00.057556164Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}